{#
	Site header — appears on every page via the base layout.
	The nav links use `page.url` (provided by 11ty) to add an "active" class
	to the link that matches the current page. The Nunjucks `in` keyword tests
	whether a string is contained within another — '/gallery' in page.url is true
	for both /gallery/ and /gallery/horsehead-nebula/.

	Mobile nav: uses a <button> toggle + JS (in base.njk) rather than the
	CSS-only checkbox trick, so keyboard and screen reader users can access it.
	JS toggles aria-expanded on the button and a nav-open class on the header.
#}
<header class="site-header" id="site-header">
	<div class="header-inner">

		{# Logo / wordmark — links back to the homepage #}
		<a href="/" class="site-logo" aria-label="DUST·IN·SPACE — Home">
			<span class="logo-dust">DUST</span><span class="logo-dot">·</span><span class="logo-in">IN</span><span class="logo-dot">·</span><span class="logo-space">SPACE</span>
		</a>

		{#
			Mobile hamburger button.
			aria-expanded tracks whether the nav is open (read by screen readers).
			aria-controls points to the nav element it opens.
			JS in base.njk wires this up.
		#}
		<button
			id="nav-toggle-btn"
			class="nav-toggle-btn"
			aria-expanded="false"
			aria-controls="site-nav"
			aria-label="Toggle navigation"
		>
			<span></span>
			<span></span>
			<span></span>
		</button>

		{#
			Nav is split into two 4-link clusters flanking the centered logo
			(2026-06-10). Why: the logo must sit at exact viewport center —
			the homepage hero title docks to left:50% — and a single
			right-aligned 8-link list reaches center on anything narrower
			than ~1800px. Splitting halves each cluster's width, so the full
			nav coexists with the centered title down to ~1300px (measured:
			the descending hero title is up to ~388px wide while crossing
			the nav band; each flank must clear half of that plus margin).

			One <nav> landmark, two <ul>s: screen readers announce a single
			"Main navigation" with two lists (4 + 4), same total links and
			same DOM/tab order as the old single list. On desktop, CSS gives
			.site-nav `display: contents` so each cluster becomes a grid
			child of .header-inner; below the collapse breakpoint .site-nav
			turns back into the dropdown panel holding both lists stacked.

			Topic split — left: the sky and the craft; right: the maker.
		#}
		<nav class="site-nav" id="site-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
			<ul class="nav-cluster nav-cluster--left">
				<li><a href="/gallery/"     {% if '/gallery'     in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Gallery</a></li>
				<li><a href="/skywatching/" {% if '/skywatching' in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Skywatching</a></li>
				<li><a href="/setup/"       {% if '/setup'       in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>My Setup</a></li>
				<li><a href="/guides/"  {% if '/guides'  in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Guides</a></li>
			</ul>
			<ul class="nav-cluster nav-cluster--right">
				<li><a href="/projects/" {% if '/projects' in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Projects</a></li>
				<li><a href="/about/"   {% if '/about'   in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>About</a></li>
				<li><a href="/store/"   {% if '/store'   in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Store</a></li>
				<li><a href="/contact/" {% if '/contact' in page.url %}class="active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Contact</a></li>
			</ul>
		</nav>

	</div>
</header>
